{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 15,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.447,
            0.97,
            0.552,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.276,
            0.086,
            0.898,
            0.118
          ],
          "content": "What does $1 0 \\mathrm { H } _ { 2 } \\mathrm { O }$ signify? Does it make $\\mathrm { N a _ { 2 } C O _ { 3 } }$ **wet? We will address this question in the next section.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.275,
            0.118,
            0.899,
            0.152
          ],
          "content": "**Sodium carbonate and sodium hydrogencarbonate are useful chemicals for many industrial processes as well.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.276,
            0.166,
            0.485,
            0.182
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Uses of washing soda"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.305,
            0.182,
            0.899,
            0.214
          ],
          "content": "**(i) Sodium carbonate (washing soda) is used in glass, soap and paper industries.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.301,
            0.214,
            0.897,
            0.23
          ],
          "content": "**(ii) It is used in the manufacture of sodium compounds such as borax.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.297,
            0.23,
            0.899,
            0.262
          ],
          "content": "**(iii) Sodium carbonate can be used as a cleaning agent for domestic purposes.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.297,
            0.262,
            0.806,
            0.279
          ],
          "content": "**(iv) It is used for removing permanent hardness of water.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.276,
            0.294,
            0.714,
            0.314
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "2.4.4 Are the Crystals of Salts really Dry?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.076,
            0.323,
            0.509,
            0.583
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.075,
                0.596,
                0.203,
                0.64
              ],
              "content": "Figure 2.9 Removing water of crystallisation"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.542,
            0.33,
            0.714,
            0.353
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 2.15"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.534,
            0.365,
            0.875,
            0.394
          ],
          "content": "**Heat a few crystals of copper sulphate in a dry boiling tube.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.535,
            0.394,
            0.876,
            0.423
          ],
          "content": "**What is the colour of the copper sulphate after heating?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.537,
            0.423,
            0.876,
            0.467
          ],
          "content": "**Do you notice water droplets in the boiling tube? Where have these come from?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.542,
            0.467,
            0.876,
            0.511
          ],
          "content": "**Add 2-3 drops of water on the sample of copper sulphate obtained after heating.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.535,
            0.51,
            0.875,
            0.541
          ],
          "content": "**What do you observe? Is the blue colour of copper sulphate restored?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.274,
            0.596,
            0.9,
            0.644
          ],
          "content": "**Copper sulphate crystals which seem to be dry contain water of crystallisation. When we heat the crystals, this water is removed and the salt turns white.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.274,
            0.644,
            0.899,
            0.676
          ],
          "content": "**If you moisten the crystals again with water, you will find that blue colour of the crystals reappears.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.274,
            0.676,
            0.9,
            0.757
          ],
          "content": "**Water of crystallisation is the fixed number of water molecules present in one formula unit of a salt. Five water molecules are present in one formula unit of copper sulphate. Chemical formula for hydrated copper** sulphate is Cu $\\mathrm{SO}_{4^{-}}^{-}\\mathrm{^{5}H_{2}O}$ **. Now you would be able to answer the question** whether the molecule of $\\mathrm{Na_{2}CO_{3}.10H_{2}O}$ **is wet.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.274,
            0.756,
            0.899,
            0.806
          ],
          "content": "**One other salt, which possesses water of crystallisation is gypsum. It has two water molecules as water of cyrstallisation. It has the chemical** formula $CaSO _{4} .2 H _{2} O$ **.** Let us look into the use of this salt."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.276,
            0.812,
            0.432,
            0.828
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Plaster of Paris"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.275,
            0.828,
            0.899,
            0.845
          ],
          "content": "**On heating gypsum at 373 K, it loses water molecules and becomes**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.276,
            0.852,
            0.901,
            0.883
          ],
          "content": "calcium sulphate hemihydrate $( \\mathrm { C a S O } _ { 4 }   . \\frac { 1 } { 2 }   \\mathrm { H } _ { 2 } \\mathrm { O }   )$ **. This is called Plaster of**"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.093,
            0.917,
            0.121,
            0.931
          ],
          "content": "32"
        },
